      "text": "At about ten at night on the 16th of May, in Gaza, the commander of the UN force was handed a letter from General Fawzi. The commander was an Indian officer, Major-General Indar Jit Rikhye. The letter said Egyptian troops were already gathered in Sinai. It asked the UN to withdraw its soldiers from their observation posts along the border. Rikhye answered that he could not move without orders from the Secretary-General, the head of the United Nations. In New York, Secretary-General U Thant required a formal request from Egypt's government. He treated the request as a decision about UNEF's continued presence, not just a few border posts. On the 18th of May Egypt's Foreign Minister, Mahmoud Riad, formally withdrew Egypt's consent for the force to stay. Egyptian troops were already blocking UN soldiers from their posts. U Thant agreed the same day. The force stood on Egyptian soil only with Egypt's consent. U Thant asked whether Israel would take UNEF on its side of the line. Israel's UN ambassador, Gideon Rafael, said that was \"entirely unacceptable\". U Thant said the force could not remain without Egypt's consent. Critics, then and since, disputed how quickly he acted. Some say he should have taken it to the General Assembly, the meeting of all UN members, or played for time. U Thant ordered withdrawal to begin on the 19th of May. UNEF did not vanish overnight. The Canadians were flown out by the 31st of May. Indian, Brazilian and Swedish soldiers were still in the area when war came."
