      "text": "At three in the morning on the 8th of June, Egyptian tanks hit an Israeli light-tank camp near Bir Gifgafa. They burned several vehicles before pulling back. On the coast road, Lieutenant-Colonel Yisrael Granit's group reached El Qantara, the first Israelis at the Canal. Near the Canal, Colonel Eitan was shot in the head and badly wounded. Aharon Davidi took over. In the south, Yoffe's forces reached the Gulf of Suez. The Egyptians set the Ras Sudar oil fields on fire. Israeli sources disagree whether Ras Sudar was taken on the 8th or the 9th. Captain David Caspi led his Centurions through the Giddi Pass, past a fresh Egyptian battalion, then raced for the Canal. He radioed that he could \"spit on the canal.\" An Israeli account places Tal's reconnaissance tanks at the Canal shortly after midnight on the 9th. By the end of that day, Israel held all of Sinai up to the Canal, except Port Fuad. On the 8th of June, Egypt notified the UN that it accepted the ceasefire, on condition that Israel also stopped firing. Israel wanted to stand on the Canal before the ceasefire took effect. Tens of thousands of Egyptian soldiers were left to walk west through the desert in the June heat. Thousands of Egyptian servicemen died in the fighting and retreat. The total is uncertain; some died from thirst and exhaustion. Amin Mohammad Darwish was on duty near the border on the 5th of June. He later recalled walking for three days, past abandoned and burned-out weapons, before he was captured. He was then held in Israel for eight months. \"I was humiliated,\" he said. After the fighting, Red Cross delegates helped Egyptian servicemen return home. Israel captured between about four thousand three hundred and five thousand Egyptian prisoners, with about five hundred officers. Many were held at Atlit for months. Egypt also held Israeli prisoners. The Red Cross records nine Israeli servicemen returning in the January 1968 exchange, and an Israeli pilot released earlier. Israel lost about two hundred and seventy-five killed and eight hundred wounded in Sinai, by General Gavish's count. The desert kept its dead for a long time. In 2024, Egyptian news reports identified remains found in North Sinai as those of Fawzy Mohammed Abdel-Muwla, a soldier killed in 1967. He was given a military funeral."
